---
/**
 * /list/{year}/ — annual list (ui-spec §4).
 *
 * Two mutually exclusive renders decided by data, not by clock:
 *  - FINALIZED: a snapshot for {year} exists → render ONLY the frozen
 *    snapshot (titles/labels/scores denormalized at finalize time — later
 *    config or score edits cannot reach this page, R-8). Preface (editor's
 *    markdown) above the list (US-1 AC3). Title never hardcodes a count —
 *    the caption carries "{n}장의 앨범" automatically (R-6).
 *  - PROGRESSIVE: {year} is the active year (or any other not-yet-finalized
 *    year, R-8) → live top10 only.
 * Covers are display-time lookups by slug (covers aren't frozen data).
 *
 * GENRE SECTIONS WERE REMOVED FROM BOTH FACES (2026-09-09, decision-maker
 * request, made the moment `config/genres.yaml` grew from 3 buckets to 8):
 * a finalized year no longer prints its "Genre Selections" section and a
 * progressive year no longer prints its per-bucket "Genre Nominees" board
 * grid — eight tiny sections, several permanently empty for any given year,
 * read as clutter under the one list a reader actually came for. This is a
 * RENDER-ONLY cut: `snapshot.data.buckets` (the frozen per-bucket
 * winner/nominees the finalize CLI writes) and `board` (this page's own
 * per-request `deriveBoard` call for a progressive year) both still carried
 * that data before this cut and — for `snapshot.data.buckets` — still do;
 * see below for which of the two this page still computes. Deleting the
 * SNAPSHOT'S data instead of just not rendering it was never on the table:
 * E-119 diffs a finalized year's `config/genres.yaml` block against its
 * snapshot's buckets to enforce the freeze (R-8), and an empty array would
 * make that comparison meaningless. `min_reviews_to_publish` (config/
 * genres.yaml) and the `published` flag it produces on each snapshot bucket
 * therefore still exist and still do real work (gating what the finalize
 * CLI writes, which the E-119 diff still needs) even though neither has any
 * effect on what a reader sees here any more — reported upstream rather than
 * touched, since config/genres.yaml's own comment describing that field is
 * outside this package's owned paths. BoardPanel/BoardRow (components) lost
 * their only caller in the same cut and were removed as dead code rather
 * than left importable-but-unused; ChartCard (home page top10 cards) is
 * unrelated and untouched.
 *
 * TITLE (2026-09-07): both faces carry the SAME single-line h1, "Charts
 * {year}", and NO overline. Every other tab on this site names itself in one
 * English line — Reviews, Notes, Artists, Archive — and this page was the
 * only one spending two lines and two languages to do it ("Charts" over
 * "{year} 올해의 앨범"). Folding the year into the section name satisfies
 * the title rules in global.css rather than carving an exception out of
 * them: rule 1 (one h1, one size) is unchanged, and rule 3 removes the
 * overline for us — an overline is only allowed where the h1 is NOT the
 * section name, and "Charts 2026" is the section name plus the axis value
 * it is scoped to, the same shape "/archive/{year}" would take if Archive
 * had a per-year tab. The Korean heading survives where it is still the
 * heading proper: the home's Charts section, which is a section on a page
 * and not a page.
 *
 * A finalized year is marked by a `.finalized-mark` chip above the title and
 * by the caption underneath, neither of which the progressive face has. The
 * chip stays Korean: it is a STATE, not a section name, and it is the label
 * on the immutability sentence directly below it.
 */
import { getEntry, render } from 'astro:content';
import ListRankRow from '../../../components/ListRankRow.astro';
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { coverSetFor } from '../../../lib/covers.ts';
import { deriveTop10 } from '../../../lib/derive/lists.ts';
import { getSiteData } from '../../../lib/derive/site-data.ts';
import { withBase } from '../../../lib/paths.ts';

// listYears (site-data.ts) is the one definition of "which years exist" —
// every year with a genres.yaml block, a snapshot, or that is active_year.
// A year with a block but zero reviews still gets a page here, rendered the
// same empty-progressive way active_year does on day one (an empty top10
// section, or — since the genre-boards cut above — no section at all when
// there is nothing to rank yet) — that IS the retroactive-chart workflow
// (R-8, 2026-09-08): stand up a past year's block, accumulate reviews
// against it build by build, watch it here, finalize whenever it is
// complete.
export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const { listYears } = getSiteData();
  return listYears.map((year) => ({ params: { year: String(year) } }));
}

const year = Number(Astro.params.year);
const { joined, data, listYears } = getSiteData();
const snapshot = data.snapshots.find((s) => s.data.year === year);
// Progressive years are derived PER PAGE, not read off the getSiteData()
// singleton — that singleton is deliberately locked to active_year alone
// (the home board's contract, ADR-0005), while this page now also renders
// any other not-yet-finalized year. deriveTop10 is a pure function of
// (joined reviews, year), so recomputing here for a year other than
// active_year is just calling it with a different argument, not a second
// implementation (same doctrine as finalize.ts §intro). deriveBoard is NOT
// called here any more (2026-09-09) — its per-bucket board was this page's
// only caller for a progressive year, and the genre-boards cut above (see
// the file intro) removed the section that rendered it. `data.genres` is
// therefore also unused on this page now, left untouched at its source
// (getSiteData) since other pages still read it.
const top10 = snapshot ? null : deriveTop10(joined, year);

const albumCovers = new Map(data.albums.map((a) => [a.slug, a.data.cover]));
const coverFor = (slug: string, title: string, artistsLabel: string) =>
  coverSetFor({ slug, title, artistsLabel, cover: albumCovers.get(slug) });

// Finalized page data (frozen strings straight from the snapshot).
let Preface: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof render>>['Content'] | null = null;
if (snapshot) {
  const entry = await getEntry('snapshots', String(year));
  if (entry && (entry.body ?? '').trim().length > 0) {
    Preface = (await render(entry)).Content;
  }
}
// `snapshot.data.buckets` (winner/nominees per genre, plus the `published`
// flag min_reviews_to_publish produced) is READ NOWHERE on this page any
// more (2026-09-09, see the file intro's GENRE SECTIONS paragraph) — it is
// not re-derived into a local const the way `top10` above is, because there
// is no longer a render site left to feed. The field itself stays on the
// snapshot's own schema/content untouched; this is one page choosing not to
// look at it, not the data going away.
const previousSnapshot = data.snapshots.find((s) => s.data.year === year - 1);

// Year nav (2026-09-08): plain links, no client JS — listYears is already
// sorted ascending, so stepping to the neighbouring index IS prev/next. At
// either end there is no year to point at, so the control is simply absent
// rather than rendered disabled — unlike the Charts pager (index.astro),
// there is no real destination behind it to announce a state for.
const yearIdx = listYears.indexOf(year);
const prevYear = yearIdx > 0 ? listYears[yearIdx - 1] : null;
const nextYear = yearIdx < listYears.length - 1 ? listYears[yearIdx + 1] : null;
---

<Base
  title={`Charts ${year}`}
  description={snapshot ? `${snapshot.data.finalized_at}에 확정된 ${year} 올해의 앨범.` : `${year} 올해의 앨범 — 현재 노미네이트.`}
  path={`/list/${year}/`}
  ogImage={`/og/list/${year}.png`}
  current="list"
  preloadSerif={Preface != null}
>
  <main class="list-page">
    {/* Prev/next year — plain anchors, no client JS (this site's one script
        budget stays spent on the Charts pager and cover zoom, not this). Each
        side is its own wrapper so `space-between` still pins the surviving
        link to its edge when the other year does not exist (oldest year has
        no prev, newest has no next) — see the intro for why an absent year
        renders nothing rather than a disabled placeholder. */}
    {(prevYear != null || nextYear != null) && (
      <nav class="year-nav" aria-label="연도 이동">
        <span>
          {prevYear != null && (
            <a class="year-nav-link" href={withBase(`/list/${prevYear}/`)}>
              <span aria-hidden="true">←</span> {prevYear}
            </a>
          )}
        </span>
        <span>
          {nextYear != null && (
            <a class="year-nav-link" href={withBase(`/list/${nextYear}/`)}>
              {nextYear} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          )}
        </span>
      </nav>
    )}
    <header class="head">
      {/* No overline — see the intro. The row survives only to carry the
          frozen-year chip, so it renders only when there is one. */}
      {snapshot && (
        <div class="state">
          <span class="finalized-mark">확정</span>
        </div>
      )}
      {/* `head-sheen` (global.css) — the second and last heading allowed the
          gradient display treatment. This page IS the list the scores
          produced, which is what --accent is reserved for (tokens.css). */}
      <h1 class="page-title head-sheen tnum">{`Charts ${year}`}</h1>
      {
        /* The progressive year has no caption (2026-09-07). "진행 중인 한 해"
           and "이 리스트는 평론이 쌓일 때마다 바뀝니다. 12월에 확정됩니다."
           were cut for the same reason the home's progress strip was: they
           narrate the publication's own state instead of the year's records,
           and a reader who has just clicked Charts is here for the albums.
           The chip above says whether the year is 확정 — the page's
           structure carries what the sentence was carrying (the "Nominees"/
           "Selections" section headings this comment used to also point to
           are gone as of 2026-09-09, see the file intro's GENRE SECTIONS
           paragraph; the chip alone still does the job for what remains).
           The finalized caption STAYS: it is not about progress, it is the
           immutability contract (R-8) plus the count R-6 forbids hardcoding
           into the title. */
        snapshot && (
          <p class="caption">
            {`${snapshot.data.finalized_at}에 확정된 리스트입니다. 이후의 평론·수정은 여기 반영되지 않습니다. ${snapshot.data.top10.length}장의 앨범.`}
          </p>
        )
      }
    </header>

    {
      snapshot ? (
        <>
          {Preface && (
            <section class="preface prose">
              <Preface />
            </section>
          )}
          <section aria-label={`${year} 올해의 앨범`}>
            <ol class="rows">
              {snapshot.data.top10.map((entry) => (
                <ListRankRow
                  rank={entry.rank}
                  title={entry.title}
                  artistsLabel={entry.artists_label}
                  score={entry.score}
                  reviewUrl={`/reviews/${entry.album}/`}
                  cover={coverFor(entry.album, entry.title, entry.artists_label)}
                  champion={entry.rank === 1}
                />
              ))}
            </ol>
          </section>
        </>
      ) : (
        // top10 is non-null here by construction (`snapshot ? null :
        // deriveTop10(...)` above) — the `!` just states that invariant to
        // TS, which cannot see across the two separate branches on its own.
        <>
          {top10!.entries.length > 0 && (
            /* No visible heading (2026-09-07). "올해의 앨범 후보 — 현재 상위
               {n}" described the page's own mechanism — what the rows below
               it are and how many there are — which is the same kind of
               self-narration this round cut from the home progress strip and
               this page's caption. The ranked rows say they are ranked, and
               the count is the length of the list. The region keeps an
               accessible name so a screen-reader reader can still jump to
               it; that name is not page copy. */
            <section aria-label="올해의 앨범 후보">
              <ol class="rows">
                {top10!.entries.map((entry, i) => (
                  <ListRankRow
                    rank={i + 1}
                    title={entry.title}
                    artistsLabel={entry.artists_label}
                    score={entry.score}
                    reviewUrl={entry.review_url}
                    cover={entry.cover}
                  />
                ))}
              </ol>
            </section>
          )}
          {previousSnapshot && (
            <a class="tail-link" href={withBase(`/list/${year - 1}/`)}>지난해 확정 리스트 →</a>
          )}
        </>
      )
    }
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .list-page {
    width: min(var(--shell), 100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--s-48) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-48);
  }
  /* Year nav — a thin wayfinding strip, not a section, so it takes a tighter
     gap to the title than `.list-page`'s 48px section rhythm (same
     margin-stacks-with-gap technique `.head` already uses below). Two
     wrapper spans keep `space-between` pinning whichever link exists to its
     own edge even when the other side is empty (see the intro). */
  .year-nav {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }
  .year-nav + .head {
    margin-top: calc(var(--s-16) - var(--s-48));
  }
  .year-nav-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-8);
    min-height: 44px;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    line-height: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .year-nav-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  /* Size and weight come from the shared .page-title (global.css); only the
     spacing this page needs is local. The top margin is the gap under the
     frozen-year chip, so it exists only when the chip does — the
     progressive face has nothing above the title to be spaced from. */
  .page-title {
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .state + .page-title {
    margin-top: var(--s-12);
  }
  .state {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--s-8);
  }
  /* TITLE→CONTENT IS THE SITE'S ONE SHARED GAP, --s-24, NOT THIS PAGE'S OWN
     --s-48 (2026-09-08). `.list-page`'s 48px `gap` above spaces its LATER
     content sections apart (preface → top10 rows → the prior-year link —
     the genre boards that used to sit in this rhythm too were cut
     2026-09-09, see the file intro) — that is this page's own rhythm and
     stays untouched. `.head` (the title block, plus the frozen-year chip
     and caption when present) is that flex column's first item, so before
     this rule the gap to whichever section renders next — preface, the
     finalized top10, or the progressive top10 — inherited the 48px
     section-rhythm number instead of the 24px every other tab uses between
     its title and its first content. (A progressive year with zero reviews
     and no prior-year link now renders nothing after `.head` at all, since
     the genre boards that used to fill that gap are gone — this rule is a
     no-op then, not a bug: there is no next sibling for it to space.)
     Margins on
     a flex item stack with `gap` (never collapse into it), so 48px + this
     rule's −24px lands that one pair on 24px without touching any gap
     further down the column. This is the active year's own page — the one
     "Charts" in the masthead actually links to — so this is the number a
     reader compares against Reviews/Notes/Artists/Archive by clicking
     between them; a FINALIZED year's frozen page keeps its immutability
     caption inside `.head` and is one of the justified detail-page
     exceptions (see e2e/tests/browser.responsive.spec.ts's mobile title-
     position test and 08-impl-notes/frontend.md for the full list). */
  .head {
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--s-24) - var(--s-48));
  }
  /* A neutral chip, not an accent plate. The accent means "the score made
     this", and while a finalized list IS the scores' final output, the page
     already spends its accent budget on the champion row's rank and rail;
     a second filled block would halve that one's weight (the same reason
     NominateBadge is an outline). --ink on --surface-3 is 12.48:1. */
  .finalized-mark {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 var(--s-8);
    min-height: 20px;
    background: var(--surface-3);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: var(--label-ls-ko);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .caption {
    margin-top: var(--s-8);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    line-height: 20px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* The preface is the editor's own writing about the year — one of the two
     surfaces where the serif survived the reskin (the other is review bodies). */
  .preface {
    max-width: var(--column);
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: var(--fs-17);
    line-height: 1.85;
  }
  .preface :global(p) {
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-24);
  }
  .rows {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* `.section-title`, `.bucket`, `.bucket-label`(.ko), `.bucket-grid` were
     removed 2026-09-09 along with the Genre Nominees/Selections markup they
     styled (see the file intro's GENRE SECTIONS paragraph) — no other
     element on this page used these classes. */
  .tail-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    font-size: var(--fs-15);
    line-height: 20px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .tail-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .list-page {
      padding-top: var(--s-32);
    }
  }
</style>
